Phase 1 - Deploy your DockerHub Image on Kubernetes

Instead of running

docker run

deploy it on Kubernetes.

Create

deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-webapp
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: yourdockerhubusername/myapp:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80

Apply

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Create Service

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-web-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: web
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
  type: NodePort
kubectl apply -f service.yaml

Access

minikube service my-web-service

Now your app is running inside Kubernetes.

Phase 2 - Scale Application
kubectl scale deployment my-webapp --replicas=5

Show

kubectl get pods

Now your resume can mention

Deployed containerized applications on Kubernetes with ReplicaSets and Deployments.

Phase 3 - Rolling Update

Update image

docker build -t yourrepo:v2 .
docker push yourrepo:v2

Then

kubectl set image deployment/my-webapp web=yourrepo:v2

Observe

kubectl rollout status deployment my-webapp

Rollback

kubectl rollout undo deployment my-webapp

Interviewers love this.

Phase 4 - Install Prometheus

Use Helm.

helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts

helm repo update

helm install prometheus prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack

This installs

Prometheus
Grafana
AlertManager
Node Exporter
kube-state-metrics

in one command.

Check

kubectl get pods -A

You'll see

