Perfect — let’s extend your Minikube Ingress setup with TLS/HTTPS so you can access your app securely in the browser.

🔑 Steps to Add TLS to Ingress
Generate a self‑signed certificate

bash
openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 \
  -keyout tls.key -out tls.crt \
  -subj "/CN=my-web-service.local/O=my-web-service"
CN must match the hostname you used in your Ingress (my-web-service.local).

Create a Kubernetes Secret

bash
kubectl create secret tls my-web-tls \
  --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key
Update your Ingress resource
Edit my-web-ingress.yaml to include TLS:

yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: my-web-ingress
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
  tls:
  - hosts:
    - my-web-service.local
    secretName: my-web-tls
  rules:
  - host: my-web-service.local
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: my-web-service
            port:
              number: 80
Apply it:

bash
kubectl apply -f my-web-ingress.yaml
Update your hosts file
Make sure you still have:

Code
<minikube-ip>   my-web-service.local
in /etc/hosts (Linux/macOS) or C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts (Windows).

Access via HTTPS
Open:

Code
https://my-web-service.local/
Your browser will warn about the self‑signed certificate. Accept/allow it, and you’ll see your app over HTTPS.

✅ Summary
You now have a TLS Ingress with a self‑signed cert.

Traffic flows: Browser → Ingress (HTTPS) → Service → Pod.

For production, you’d replace the self‑signed cert with a real one from Let’s Encrypt or another CA.
